  return Array(2);
}
What it really returned: returns [null,null]
Earlier they asked: When you start with just a number, say 5, and make a new list from it, what do you get back (five empty spots, or something filled in?), and what do you usually do with that list right after: fill each spot, loop over it, or something else?
You said: With a single number I get a list of that length with nothing actually in it; asking for 2 gave me back [null,null], two empty holes that only print as null, so 5 would give five empty spots. I almost always fill it right away (with a default value or by mapping over indexes), because empty holes get skipped by most loops and are not useful on their own.
Their question: Say you make that five-spot list and then add one new value to the end before filling anything. Does the new value land in a sixth spot after the five empty ones, or does it take the first empty spot? In other words, is the number you gave the actual length of the list, or just room set aside for later?

### Array() #3
The code step is the JavaScript call Array().
A real example of it:
function Array_call_3() {
  return Array(1, 2);
}
What it really returned: returns [1,2]
Earlier they asked: When you hand over a few values, say 3, 'a' and true, and get back a new list holding them, what is that list for in your code, and is it a fresh list made from scratch, or a copy of a list you already had?
You said: With more than one value it just builds a list holding exactly those values in order; handing it 1 and 2 gave back [1,2], so 3, 'a' and true would give a three-item list of those. It is a brand-new list made from scratch out of the values I passed, not a copy of an existing list, and I use it as a small ready-made collection to pass along or loop over.
Their question: If you hand over just one value that is not a number, say only 'a', do you get a one-item list holding 'a'? And when would you build a list this way instead of simply writing the values out as a list yourself? What does doing it this way give you?

### Array.[Symbol.species]
